Date: Sun, 13 Jun 1993 02:49:51 -0400 (EDT)
From: esr@snark.thyrsus.com (Eric S. Raymond)
Subject: BOOK:CONSCIOUSNESS AND POLITICS

> Actually this is an outline of the book I'm
> writing Consciousness and Politics.

An attempt at constructive criticism follows...

> EPISTEMOLOGY: (the nature of and means of seeking truth) 
> Today science and philosophy suggest that there is no
> ultimate, unchanging truth.  Physicists note that merely
> observing reality changes it.  Some suggest that "natural
> laws" are not static and unchanging but ever-evolving. 
> Logicians point out the impossibility of constructing
> logically consistent arguments.  It seems neither God nor
> natural law has created some objective, knowable reality.  

This paragraph implies numerous technical errors and a dangerous rhetorical one.

"no ultimate, unchanging truth": There is a technical sense in which this
is certainly true.  The trouble with putting it as you have here, though,
is that you make it sound as though we are bereft not only of truths in
the Aristotelian classical sense but also of truths in the predictive/
pragmatic sense.  And the latter is certainly false.

"Physicists note that merely observing reality changes it."  Again, this
is misleading.  It is not "observation" that collapses the wave function,
but *interaction*.  All present modes of observation *depend* on interaction,
but that may change soon of the Deutsch experiment and reversible computing
are actually implemented.  That is, it may soon become possible to observe in
the quantum sense without irreversable change in the observed system.

The reason this distinction is important is that "observation" implies an
observer, whereas "interaction" avoids talking about anything but the
actual mathematics of scattering, etc.  The Copenhagen interpretation has
done a great deal of mischief by confusing these two concepts; you should
avoid perpetuating its errors.

"Some suggest that natural laws are not static and unchanging but ever-
evolving"  Nope.  All those theories with changing gravitational constants
and the like are pretty much in the dumper at this point.  Human
*understanding* of natural law evolves, but it's wrong to imply that the
universe is routinely changing the rules on us.

>      Even if there was an objective reality, humans would be
> incapable of knowing it objectively.  Humans more "construct"
> reality and truth than "discover" them.  Each one of us views
> reality through a unique "psychic grid" influenced by
> society's teachings and our personal experience.  

"Even if there was an objective reality" ... But there is one.  If you doubt
it, walk off a cliff and wish yourself out of falling.  If you succeed, bravo.
If you go splat...well, the world didn't really need yet another failed
philosopher, did it?

You've made a common error.  You've assumed that the refutation of classical,
objectivist metaphysics implies subjectivism.  It doesn't.  For a clear
exposition of this in accessible terms, read George Lakoff's "Women, Fire and
Dangerous Things".

Besides being wrong, subjectivism is dangerous.  If thought is ontologically
(rather than just epistemically) prior to reality, then will is prior to
reality and *everything is politics*.  Believe me, you don't want to live
in such a world!

>      Despite the seeming relativism of truth, humans must
> endeavor to discover the most accurate knowledge of the nature
> of reality and human purpose.

Good...

>                               And we must learn how to best
> combine the three means to knowledge--intuition, reason and
> empirical methods--to do so.

You have the proper order reversed.   Intuition only works in situations
where neurology and evolution has pre-equipped us with a good set of
basic-level categories.  That works for dealing with other humans, and
for throwing things, and for a bunch of other things that do not,
unfortunately, include constructing viable philosophies.

>                              Moreover, knowing that we create
> our own "psychic grids", each of us must learn to create our
> own reality instead of just going along with what the social,
> economic and political "powers-that-be" call reality.    

Good.  

> METAPHYSICS:  (the nature and purpose of reality)   New
> paradigm science describes principles of self-organizing which
> operate all through reality--from the sub-atomic, chemical,
> astronomical, and planetary levels, up into the cellular,
> plant, animal and human levels.

Yes.  Have you been reading Prigogine?

>                                Scientists describe differing
> but complimentary dynamic and integrative principles which
> resemble the "yang" and "yin" described in Taoist philosophy. 

This looks like it's haring off into an unhelpful sort of mysticism.
Taoism is wonderful for reprogramming your mind --- not very good for
understanding nature, though.  For a good laugh, read up on Taoist
alchemy sometime.

> And they see evidence of an inherent drive to evolve more
> organized systems and more fully conscious beings.  

More organized systems, yes.  That's what Prigogine's analysis of
dissipative systems teaches us.   But the second half sounds like
unhelpful mysticism again.

>      Many scientists believe these principles of self-
> organization and evolution are much like consciousness.  They
> notice that dynamic and integrative principles act like
> imagination, memory and will.  Moreover, there is evidence of
> fields of awareness and memory called "morphogenetic" fields
> linking similar entities--atoms, chemicals, species of plants
> and animals.  These fields communicate knowledge among, and
> aid in the physical and social evolution of, each type or
> species.  

Ummm...you are wandering off into very fringey, unverified stuff here.
Lovelock doesn't buy the Gaia-is-conscious riff, and I don't know of
any reputable scientists who thinks Sheldrake is anything but a nut.
When the "morphogenetic field" hypothesis makes predictions that are
experimentally verified at a high confidence level, then scientists
will take it seriously.

Until then, best start this paragraph not with "Many scientists believe"
but with "A few scientists speculate".

>      Because these principles are like consciousness, and
> because their purpose seems to be greater consciousness, I
> personally choose to call the very nature and purpose of
> reality "consciousness."

It is not established that they are "like consciousness" at all --- there's no
teleology in Prigoginian thermodynamics at all, and that's the only global
self-organizing principle that's stood an experimental test as yet.

>                                   Further, I emphasize that the
> concept of consciousness must replace the concept of god.  For
> if humans recognize we are the product of our own conscious
> evolution, not the creation of a transcendent god, we will
> recognize ourselves as the creators of human destiny and
> reject authoritarians who claim to speak and act in the name
> of some superior being.   

While I agree with your ethical aims here, I think you're going to
play into the hands of theists and other obnoxious sorts of irrationalist
if you try to make "consciousness" the center of your universe-picture.
There's no good evidence that the universe works that way, and the
claim leaves you vulnerable to co-option by people who want to
resurrect the Nobodaddy-god.

The rest moves away from philosophy into realms I'm less competent to
critique.  I hope this was some help.
-- 
					Eric S. Raymond <esr@snark.thyrsus.com>

