Well...it's a well-established fact that sunlight on (white) skin boosts
vitamin D levels (I don't know that anyone has tested this on blacks or
Asiatics; possibly not).  

Either (a) white people got paler skin to take advantage of this, or (b)
it's pure coincidence that they diverged from the human norm in that useful
way.  I don't believe in such coincidences, not on evolutionary timescales.

About dairy-farming I dunno.  It's certainly possible that both explanations
are correct --- that is, dairying originally evolved as a scarcity response,
but developed a more specific nutritional role for early Europeans.

Your account of the origins of dairying has a testable consequence; lactose-
intolerance ought to be rarer in Middle-Eastern populations than in
European ones.  Don't be surprised if it isn't; Europeans may well have been
dairying longer than the historical Middle Eastern cultures (Stonehenge is
older than the Pyramids, you know).
 
