Date: Sun, 13 Jun 1993 12:04:24 -0400 (EDT)
From: esr@snark.thyrsus.com (Eric S. Raymond)
Subject: DIET: Veggies, threat or menace? :-)

> Actually, I've heard of something precisely along these lines:
> "natural" plants have more chemical defenses (many of the carcinogenic
> in humans) than "supermarket" plants. (I think it was studied by the
> UC Berekeley prof who specializes in plant carcinogens, the one who
> has shown plants we eat every day have hundred of times the
> carcinogens of "bad" things like overcooked bacon, etc.)

That's right.  The man's name is Bruce Ames, and he devised the now-standard
Ames test for carcinogenicity.  His work is one of my major reasons for
believing that nearly everything the vegetarian/environmentalist/self-panicker
axis thinks it knows about nutrition and environmental toxins is wrong.

You hit two of his major points above.  A third implication is that humans,
like other animals, have evolved mechanisms for dealing with levels of
environmental toxins that swamp the EPA's "safety" levels --- otherwise
we'd all be dead.

This in turn cuts right to the heart of the most serious technical dispute
in toxicology --- how you extrapolate the mortality/morbidity effects of
small doses of a toxin from the huge overdoses they feed the lab rats.

U.S. law and self-panicker mythology mandates a linear extrapolation; this
has the effect that *anything* can be defined as a toxin, providing you ram
enough of it down a test animal's throat or into its bloodstream.

Ames is willing to say in public (and has backed up with his work) what a lot
of toxicologists think in private --- that this is superstitious bullshit.  The
right model, for anything besides instant-kills like fast neurotoxins, is
exponential fall-off cropped by a threshold at the low end (the threshold
represents the capacity of the body's evolved metabolic scrubbers, whatever
they are).

In other words, the contemporary folk model of the human body as a fragile
system with no defenses against lethal and carcinogenic assault by all manner of
unknown substances is completely wrong.  And the corollary that our health is
best protected by zealous toxin-inquisitors empowered to trash any industry
with putatively harmful emissions is also wrong --- the human contribution to
the environmental-toxin inventory is, comparatively, so tiny it's lost in the
noise.

For more on this, look up Ames's published works.  As one of the founders
of modern toxicology, he can't be ignored --- though the mainstream media
has certainly tried.  The environmentally-correct hate him with a passion.
-- 
					Eric S. Raymond <esr@snark.thyrsus.com>

