Date: Sun, 13 Jun 93 12:52:30 -0700
From: dasher@netcom.com (D. Anton Sherwood)
Subject: the "Lungfish" strategy

[I wrote the following in '87, I think, after reading David Brin's story 
collection THE RIVER OF TIME.]

"Lungfish" is especially mind-expanding.  It got me thinking about strategies 
for exploring the Galaxy by proxy.

The fundamental concept is several decades old.  You send a `Von Neumann' probe 
to a nearby star.  When it gets there, it beams home a preliminary survey of the 
system, builds copies of itself to send to the next few stars, and then beams 
back more detailed survey data for as long as it lives.  Von Neumann figured 
this process could explore the galaxy in a few million years.

"Lungfish" postulates that many civilizations have done this, with variations.  
Two alliances of probes are mentioned, called Loyalists (including Seekers, 
Seeders and Greeters) and Rejectors (Gobblers, Berserkers, Anti-Makers -- their 
makers must have had override codes).

There's more to the story than this, but the Rejectors got me thinking.  If 
Anti-Makers (which attack intelligence by hunting modulated radio waves) may be 
loose, we don't want our probes beaming spotlights at us.  [Was it Keith Henson 
who warned of "information overload"?]  Backtracking is a little harder, but 
still possible, if the probes beam to their neighbors and act as relays (I'll 
call this `netcasting').  Broadcasting makes backtracking impossible, but lights 
up a sphere of unusual activity; killers would aim at its center.

There's also the danger that a killer could raid a probe's memory (Niven, "A 
Teardrop Falls").  So each child probe is made ignorant : it `knows' only what 
neighbors it has, and its `Radius' = how far it and its ancestors have traveled 
on the zigzag path from Sol.  Astronomy is done in coordinates based on the 
nearest star and/or the Core.  For netcasting, each relay translates into its 
own coordinates (Solar coords are dangerous for obvious reasons; Leinster, 
"First Contact").

If probes relay and store all information, the boundary of the Explored Sphere 
can be inferred; so they ignore messages originating outside a small sphere, so 
that for each the `known universe' is symmetrical.  (A netcaster relays to each 
star within half the radius of its own small sphere.)  Each message gives its 
sender's Radius; each probe remembers the highest Radius it has heard of, and 
subtracts its own to determine its small radius.  Thus each small sphere is 
tangent to the Explored Sphere -- as big as it can safely be.

But as killers penetrate the Explored Sphere, they can find Sol from the ratios 
(rationes?) between the Radii or small radii of four or more probes.  Now what?

As Heisenberg said, you can't learn much without revealing your presence.

I wonder whether it might be a good idea to design into the probes some capacity 
to mutate.  An occasional fatal mutation is only a slight setback, because of 
the inherent redundancy of probe propagation: each probe tries to make sure that 
each nearby star has a resident probe.  If the probes reach relativistic speeds, 
each star can expect to be visited by about three, within a few years of each 
other; they might engage in `sex' before the redunds move on.

If a probe loses the ability to recognize its cousins as such, it's likely to 
try to eat them, as a source of refined metals for its own children.  Such a 
cancer might be the source of Brin's Gobblers.

