HEADLINE: 'Built-In Vulnerability'; SUPPORT CONTINUES TO ERODE FOR
GOVT. ENCRYPTION STANDARD, HOUSE PANEL TOLD

BODY:

Panel of telecommunications security experts told House Telecom
Subcommittee hearing Wed. that proposed Clipper Chip encryption
standard (CD April 17 p1) could have adverse effect on nation's
computer and telecommunications industry because agencies would have
to regulate manufacturers heavily to ensure compliance. That could put
U.S. industry at competitive disadvantage, they said. Other panel on
network security said how phone fraud is being taken over by organized
crime, with some moving out of drugs because there's less chance of
being caught and prosecuted.

Panel of experts criticized Clipper program on several fronts: (1)
Lack of public debate before program was put forward by govt. (2)
Adverse effects on exports. (3) Concerns on privacy safeguards. As
proposed, 3 unique Clipper encryption keys would be issued for each
chip made.  Once chip would be installed in device and 2 others --
called "escrow keys" -- would go to govt. agencies that would hold
them for safekeeping. Upon receiving court approval for wiretap, law
enforcement agencies would retrieve escrow keys, which in tandem would
make it possible to listen to encrypted conversations.

Of all security experts testifying, only Georgetown U. Prof. Dorothy
Denning supported Clipper program as it stands. But when asked for
recommendations on which govt. agencies should be considered escrow
key holders, she said had none. When asked if National Security Agency
(NSA) would be good choice, she said: "I don't think the public has
enough confidence in them." How about FBI? "I think that [choice]
could be viewed as a potential conflict of interest," Denning said,
her answer drawing laughter from audience.

Sun Microsystems engineer Whitfield Diffle (who invented concept of
public keys for encryption software) told Subcommittee Chmn.  Markey
(D-Mass.) that his company's products were likely to be met with
skepticism in foreign markets because of Clipper's "built-in
guaranteed vulnerability for law enforcement agencies." He said no one
was going to want to buy from Sun, even if they thought there might be
some sort of Clipper device inside -- whether or not that was case --
because "no company wants a device the U.S. government might be
listening in on." Diffle said Clipper chip's housing, which is
tamper-proof, would add appreciable cost to secure products, placing
another burden on industry. When selling to govt. agencies that
mandated use of Clipper chips, he said such products would be subject
to NSA design approval. If this happened, "the nation's computer
manufacturers would be trapped in a regulatory web more confining than
any we have seen so far."

Clipper program also might lead to "perverse" reaction, said Stephen
Bryen, pres., Secured Communications and State Dept. under secy. for
export control in Reagan Administration. He said criminals who are
targets of wiretapping using new standard actually would be encouraged
to import foreign made encryption devices that U.S. govt.  couldn't
break. Such scenario isn't far-fetched, he said: "The U.S. has no
import controls on encryption technology." In his work in Reagan
Administration, Bryen said greatest amount of encryption products
imported to U.S. were from Switzerland.

Administration has failed to "count the costs" of program, said Marc
Rotenberg, Washington dir. of Computers Professionals for Social
Responsibility (CPSR). "We should reassess the costs on privacy,
business and society," he said. "We have to ask ourselves if those
balance the needs put forth by the Clipper proposal, and we don't
think they do." Rotenberg also pointed out that under Computer
Security Act of 1987, National Institute of Standards & Technology
(NIST) -- not NSA -- was to be responsible for development of
technical standards, but NSA took leading role. In 1989, NIST set out
to develop public key cryptography standard that would be "public,
unclassified . . . usable by federal agencies and U.S.-based
multinationals," Rotenberg said, but Clipper proposal is 180 degrees
from that, according to documents CPSR obtained under Freedom of
Information Act.

Hearing's 2nd panel said organized crime is now running nation's most
lucrative telecommunications fraud rings. N.Y. State Police Senior
Investigator Donald Delaney said: "We've interviewed criminals from
organized crime operations that say they've moved from drugs to
telephone fraud because there's more money in it and less chance of
being caught." Michael Guidry, pres. of Guidry Group, private security
firm that works with Cellular Telecommunications antifraud task force,
told Committee that he had received calls from foreign law enforcement
officials asking for help on telephone fraud because organized crime
is moving in.

Telephone fraud has gone beyond "minuscule" efforts of teenage
computer hackers, said John Haugh, chmn. of Telecommunications
Advisers consulting firm that tracks phone fraud. He said FCC has
"done essentially nothing to jawbone major segments" of industry into
becoming more protective. "While the agency's attitude has been
apathetic and must change, the FCC is understaffed and underfunded,"
he said. Markey, taking up Haugh's comments, criticized Commission for
"doing nothing" after holding high-profile "all-day" hearing on phone
fraud problem. "They've just put all that information on the shelf,"
he said. FCC will be asked to give accounting of why it hasn't acted
in some way to help stem problem, Markey said, when it's called to
testify in near future.

Haugh presented dramatic statistic on losses from fraud -- up to $5
billion annually. But when pressed, he admitted figure was merely
estimate, saying there's no accurate accounting of fraud losses
because companies are hesitant to admit problem and carriers aren't
required by any law to publish such numbers, so loss data continue to
be inflated artificially. Computer search by Communications Daily
showed that in more than 50 articles published in 1992, industry
itself often quoted "experts" as saying phone fraud losses were
costing U.S.  "up to $2 billion annually."

