HEADLINE: The FBI's computer chip

BODY:

 

Not content with the advancing technology or established
constitutional law,

the FBI and National Security Agency are pushing for encrypted control
over people's private lives. Call it technological eavesdropping, and
wonder why President Clinton, who has trumpeted the dawn of new global
superinformation highways and technological superiority, would want
any part of it. Leaving privacy aside for a moment, what the security
agencies want is to put limits on the growth of new technology. They
fret that new computer data lines and high-tech communications systems
will make the world of surveillance more difficult. They may be right,
but that would also have been an argument for banning the telegraph or
the telephone. Not only is the attempt to limit technology naive, but
it also runs counter to the president's own stated national goals. As
Vice President Al Gore said recently: "The existence of a

premier information marketplace in the United States will give our
companies

and our citizens an advantage in worldwide competition."

The footnote, apparently, is that the marketplace and the technology
will be controlled by the FBI.

This emerging security strategy is also frightening in a
constitutional

sense. It goes beyond technological eavesdropping. The White House
already has

released the government's own telephone scrambling device, the so-
called

" Clipper chip, " and is hinting that it may ask for laws that require

companies to use it and ban the use of other encryption codes. What
would that

mean? Whitfield Diffie, cryptographer at Sun Microsystems Inc., told
the

Washington Post that the Clipper is akin "to the little keyhole in the
back of the combination locks used on the lockers of schoolchildren.
The children open the locks with the combinations, which is supposed
to keep the other children out, but the teachers can always look in
the lockers by using the key."

The teacher, in Diffie's analogy, is the FBI, an organization whose
history is not characterized by benevolent restraint.

Though law enforcement agencies deserve the tools to compete with the 
technology being used by criminals, they cannot be allowed to control
the communications links. The NSA may characterize the issue as one of
national security, but the civilian president, Bill Clinton, ought to
recognize the more compelling constitutional concerns. Law-abiding
citizens are entitled to a right to privacy, and allowing the FBI to
hold the encrypted key to their phone conversations and computer
transmissions is but a technological assault on their basic liberty.

