HEADLINE: Panel Sees Flaws in Plan For Encoding

BYLINE: By JOHN MARKOFF, Special to The New York Times

DATELINE: SAN FRANCISCO, June 4

BODY:

A Commerce Department advisory committee issued two draft resolutions
today raising "serious concerns" about a Clinton Administration plan
to standardize a high-technology coding system that would permit
law-enforcement officials to tap telephone calls and computer data
transmissions.

The resolutions call on the Administration to hold extensive public
hearings on the new technology, known as the Clipper chip, and they
urge that the technology not be deployed beyond the executive branch
of the Government until the public review is completed.

The Computer System Security and Privacy Advisory Board, which was
established by Congress under the Computer Security Act of 1987, held
two days of public hearings this week to solicit industry and public
reaction to the plan, which would create a national standard for
telephone and data coding.

 A 'Master Key'

The system would permit law-enforcement officials to create a numeric
"master key" to be held in "escrow." It could be obtained only by
presenting a warrant to two separate agencies that would be charged
with protecting pieces of the key. The idea is that splitting the key
would make it more difficult for Government officials to abuse its
powers.

But the proposal has generated widespread criticism from technology
experts, industry officials and privacy advocates who argue that it is
technically flawed and that it may be abused by Government officials
and others. A number of industry executives and opponents of the plan
said that today's resolution was a setback for the Government.

"This is a victory for the private sector and the privacy community,"
said Marc Rotenberg, Washington director of the Computer Professionals
for Social Responsibility, a computer industry public interest group.
"The board heard a strong set of witnesses this week saying, 'Don't do
it.' "

A White House official, who spoke on the condition of anonymity, said
today that the Administration was comfortable with the resolution and
that the Government was not rushing to put its plan into effect.

The plan calls for the use of several chips, known as Clipper and
Capstone, designed by the National Security Agency to encode voice and
data conversations, but it will not outlaw the use of other coding
systems. This has led critics of the plan to argue that the proposed
technology will not be useful in listening to people suspected of
criminal activities who wish to protect their conversations. The
advisory board says that it sees these shortcomings in the plan:

*The Administration has not made a persuasive argument that a
significant problem exists.

*Because it is a hardware-based solution, the Clipper/Capstone
proposal does not address the needs of the software industry.

*The economic implications of the proposal have not been examined.

*Congress, as well as the Administration, should play a role in the
conduct and approval of the review. Some industry officials have
suggested that the Clipper proposal may backfire on the chip's
designer, the National Security Agency, a secretive intelligence arm
of the Pentagon.

"This could be a huge mistake for the National Security Agency," said
Stephen D. Bryen, president of Delta Tech, which makes data coding
gear. "Clipper will make it more difficult for the U.S. to do what it
has to do on an international level, to deal with bad guys using
coding gear."

